'''''Eragrostis minor''''', the '''little lovegrass''' or '''smaller stinkgrass''', is a widespread species of flowering plant in the family [[Poaceae]], native to most of the subtropical and warm temperate Old World, and introduced to North America, South America, and Australia.<ref name="POWO_401317-1" >{{cite POWO |id=401317-1 |title=''Eragrostis minor'' Host |access-date=2 August 2023 }}</ref><ref name="MnWi" >{{cite web |url=https://www.minnesotawildflowers.info/grass-sedge-rush/little-lovegrass |title=''Eragrostis minor'' (Little Lovegrass) |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2023 |website=MinnesotaWildflowers.info |publisher=Minnesota Wildflowers |access-date=2 August 2023 |quote=Also known as: Smaller Stinkgrass }}</ref> Preferring disturbed open places with little competition, and sandy or gravelly soils, it is often found growing on rail embankments, road verges, cracks in sidewalks, and waste areas.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.illinoiswildflowers.info/grasses/plants/love_grass.html |title=Lesser Love Grass |last1=Hilty |first1=John |date=20 November 2019 |website=illinoiswildflowers.info |publisher=Illinois Wildflowers |access-date=2 August 2023 }}</ref> Its seeds are edible, but quite small and difficult to harvest and handle, so it is usually regarded as a [[famine food]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://tropical.theferns.info/viewtropical.php?id=Eragrostis+minor |title=Useful Tropical Plants ''Eragrostis minor'' |last1=Fern |first1=Ken |date=20 July 2021 |website=tropical.theferns.info |publisher=Tropical Plants Database |access-date=2 August 2023 }}</ref>
